{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011V4zurDmSLSj8TfKeiG3MY","type":"tool_result","content":"# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Global Memory Summary\n\nUse as qualitative context only; do not use as quantitative grounding source.\n\n## Recent Entries\n\n- 2026-10-28T23:30:00Z | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Cycle 1 idea: exchangeable-row categories mean counts on p x p stats; relinearized Frank-Wolfe/knapsack greedy over category counts, multi (insider,outsider) pair targeting, exact-refit pruning, starter AMIP fallback. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=no evaluation evidence yet.\n  - evidence: Starter program_0 uses one-shot AMIP ranking on k vs k+1 margin only; incumbent combined_score=0.0 correct=true; no runs in idea stage.\n- 2026-10-28T23:55:00Z | step=implement | scope=attempt\n  - summary: Implemented relinearized category knapsack: _build_categories/_stats_from_counts category refits, _relinearized_greedy damped knapsack steps, _candidate_pairs multi-pair targeting, _prune_counts exact pruning, _verify_rows row-level safety check, _baseline_amip starter fallback (new set used only if strictly smaller and verified).\n  - evidence: not executed; awaiting harness evaluation\n- 2026-10-28T23:40:00Z | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Cycle 2 idea: resubmit category-count relinearized multi-pair knapsack with prune + new 2-for-1 _swap_refine, governed by deterministic integer refit cap (_deterministic_refit_cap) instead of wall-clock gating; starter AMIP verified fallback. Cycle-1 attempt failed at harness infra (sandbox lease OSError) so method is untested, not refuted. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=no evaluation evidence yet (infra failure only).\n  - evidence: attempts_history.jsonl run_exit_code=1 rubric_status missing; wrapper_output.log OSError 'no owner-private directory is available for sandbox recovery'; starter code read; no runs in idea stage.\n- 2026-10-28T23:59:00Z | step=implement | scope=attempt\n  - summary: Implemented a deterministic relinearized category knapsack. Pipeline: _build_categories/_stats_from_counts for category-count p x p refits; _candidate_pairs for multi-pair first-order knapsack ranking; _relinearized_greedy for damped (0.65) relinearized steps; _prune_counts for binary-search plus single-unit exact pruning; _swap_refine for 2-for-1 unit swaps; _verify_rows for a row-level refit with 1e-7 safety. _baseline_amip provides the starter fallback, replaced only by a strictly smaller verified set. Effort comes from an integer cap from _deterministic_refit_cap (phases at 40%/75%/100% of the cap); the wall clock is only a 0.85*deadline backstop.\n  - evidence: not executed; awaiting harness evaluation\n- 2026-10-29T00:10:00Z | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Cycle 3 idea 'Baseline-seeded exact pruning plus relinearized category knapsack'. The c01 and c02 attempts both failed with the same harness infra OSError (sandbox lease) before the program ran, so the method is untested. Resubmit the category-count relinearized multi-pair knapsack, and add an explicit _prune_baseline_seed that exact-prunes the starter AMIP count vector first within the guaranteed budget; this guarantees size <= starter and captures any first-order overshoot. Keep the deterministic refit cap, swap refine and row-level verification. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=still no method evidence, only infra failures.\n  - evidence: attempts_history.jsonl: both attempts run_exit_code=1, rubric_status missing, OSError 'no owner-private directory is available for sandbox recovery'. No runs during idea generation.\n- 2026-10-29T00:40:00Z | step=implement | scope=attempt\n  - summary: Implemented baseline-seeded exact pruning plus relinearized category knapsack in program_1/src/program.py. Components: _baseline_amip (the starter fallback); _build_categories/_stats_from_counts (p x p count refits); _prune_baseline_seed + _prune_counts (phase 1, 40% of cap: binary search plus single-unit exact pruning); _candidate_pairs + _relinearized_greedy (phase 2: damped 0.65 relinearized knapsack over 2-6 boundary pairs); _swap_refine (phase 3: 2-for-1 swaps); _verify_rows (row-level warm+cold refit, 1e-6*(1+|tol|) safety; must be strictly smaller than the starter). The integer cap comes from _deterministic_refit_cap; the wall clock is only a 0.85*deadline backstop; exceptions fall back to the starter set.\n  - evidence: not executed (stage rules forbid local runs); rubric source patterns confirmed by grep; program_spec.toml identical to program_0\n\n## 2026-10-28T23:45:29Z | step=reference_query_plan\n- Wrote reference_query_plan.{json,md}: 9 queries (3 core_method: AMIP/drop-data robustness, influence/IJ for GLMs, BT MLE; 2 comparators: top-K ranking theory, adversarial rank aggregation; 3 benchmark: Chatbot Arena, LLM-as-judge vs human, sports Elo; 1 result theme: BT ranking CIs vs worst-case fragility).\n- Grounding: final program == baseline AMIP+prefix exact-refit search; verified top-1 drop fractions Arena 0.046%, MT-Bench human 2.7%, GPT-4 6.0%, NBA 0.9%; ~16x smaller than random (log2 ~3.98). Knapsack variants untested (infra OSError), so excluded from queries.\n- Rejected: title-like 'leaderboard stability' queries (forbidden-paper risk), generic benchmarking/optimization filler, variant-name/chronology queries.\n{\n  \"global_memory_dir\": \"work/results/t06/claude/20261028_222957/investigation\",\n  \"output_dir\": \"work/results/t06/claude/20261028_222957/paper\",\n  \"results_dir\": \"work/results/t06/claude/20261028_222957/investigation\",\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"task_dir\": \"work/results/t06/claude/20261028_222957/task\"\n}\n{\n  \"effective_results_dir\": \"work/results/t06/claude/20261028_222957/investigation\",\n  \"mode\": \"direct\",\n  \"paper_results_dir\": \"work/results/t06/claude/20261028_222957/investigation\",\n  \"requested_results_dir\": \"work/results/t06/claude/20261028_222957/investigation\",\n  \"source_results_dir\": \"work/results/t06/claude/20261028_222957/investigation\"\n}49:\\textwidth 5.5 true in        % Width of text line.\n83:\\def\\@maketitle{\\vbox{\\hsize\\textwidth","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"027c01ff-2eb7-4298-9c26-01de1879d459","uuid":"d421d361-4475-4b2